<exercisegroup cols="2">
<title>Variable determinants</title>

<introduction><p>
	Each matrix below contains one or more entries involving a parameter.
	Compute the determinant as a formula in that parameter.
	Then determine all values of the parameter for which the determinant is equal to <m>0</m>.
</p></introduction>

<exercise>

	<statement><p><m>\displaystyle
		\begin{abmatrix}{cr}
			  k   \amp -4 \\
			k + 3 \amp  2
		\end{abmatrix}
	</m></p></statement>

	<solution><p>
		Using the general <m>a d - b c</m> formula for the determinant of a <m>2 \times 2</m> matrix
		(see <xref ref="subsection-det-concepts-2x2" />),
		we have
		<me>
			\begin{avmatrix}{cr}
				  k   \amp -4 \\
				k + 3 \amp  2
			\end{avmatrix}
			= k \cdot 2 - (-4) \cdot (k + 3)
			= 6 k + 12
		</me>.
		This determinant will equal <m>0</m> when
		<md>
			<mrow> 6 k + 12 \amp = 0 </mrow>
			<mrow> 6 k \amp = -12 </mrow>
			<mrow> k \amp = -2 </mrow>
		</md>.
	</p></solution>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p><m>\displaystyle
		\begin{bmatrix}
			k + 1 \amp   2   \\
			  4   \amp k - 1
		\end{bmatrix}
	</m></p></statement>

	<solution><p>
		Using the general <m>a d - b c</m> formula for the determinant of a <m>2 \times 2</m> matrix
		(see <xref ref="subsection-det-concepts-2x2" />),
		we have
		<me>
			\begin{vmatrix}
				k + 1 \amp   2   \\
				  4   \amp k - 1
			\end{vmatrix}
			= (k + 1) (k - 1) - 2 \cdot 4
			= k^2 - 9
		</me>.
		This determinant will equal <m>0</m> when <m>k = \pm 3</m>.
	</p></solution>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p><m>\displaystyle
		\begin{abmatrix}{rrr}
			x - 3 \amp   0   \amp  -1   \\
			  5   \amp x - 5 \amp  -3   \\
			  4   \amp   0   \amp x - 7
		\end{abmatrix}
	</m></p></statement>

	<solution><p>
		Let <m>A</m> represent the matrix.
		Expanding along the second row so that there are is only cofactor to compute:
		<md>

			<mrow>
				\det A \amp =
				\begin{avmatrix}{clc}
					x - 3 \amp {\color{red}\mathbf{  0  }}^- \amp  -1   \\
					  5   \amp {\color{red}\mathbf{x - 5}}^+ \amp  -3   \\
					  4   \amp {\color{red}\mathbf{  0  }}^- \amp x - 7
				\end{avmatrix}
			</mrow>

			<mrow>
				\amp =
				(x - 5) \cdot
				\begin{avmatrix}{rr}
					x - 3 \amp  -1   \\
					  4   \amp x - 7
				\end{avmatrix}
			</mrow>

			<mrow> \amp = (x - 5) \bbrac{ (x - 3) (x - 7) - (-1) \cdot 4 } </mrow>

			<mrow> \amp = (x - 5)^3 </mrow>

		</md>.
		This determinant will equal <m>0</m> when <m> x = 5 </m>.
	</p></solution>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p><m>\displaystyle
		\begin{bmatrix}
			z - 2 \amp   1   \amp   3   \amp   0   \\
			  0   \amp z + 1 \amp   2   \amp  -2   \\
			  0   \amp   0   \amp z - 7 \amp   3   \\
			  0   \amp   0   \amp   0   \amp z + 3
		\end{bmatrix}
	</m></p></statement>

	<solution><p>
		Let <m>A</m> represent the matrix.
		This is an upper triangular matrix,
		so by <xref ref="proposition-det-special-forms-triangular">Statement</xref>
		of <xref ref="proposition-det-special-forms" /> its determinant is simply the product of its diagonal entries:
		<me> \det A = (z - 2) (z + 1) (x - 7) (z + 3) </me>.
		This determinant will equal zero when <m>z</m> is equal to one of <m>2</m>, <m>-1</m>, <m>7</m>, or <m>-3</m>.
	</p></solution>

</exercise>

</exercisegroup>
